; respond.eval - THE CHECK THAT THE NUMBERS STILL MOVE.
;
; Run by `Eval.ps1 -SelfTest`, not listed as a suite: it is a check, not a
; measurement, and nothing it prints is a number to compare across knob changes.
; Same reasoning as resettest.eval.
;
; WHY IT EXISTS. Everything else in -SelfTest checks the RUNNER: a bad script
; exits 1, a missing one exits 2, `reset` equals a new game, batching and
; headless change nothing. Every one of those is plumbing. Not one of them asks
; whether the TALLY still describes the fight — so the harness could report ten
; green suites forever with a counter that had gone dead, and the audit that
; found this (docs/eval-audit.md F1) is the only reason anybody looked.
;
; THE METHOD. Three pairs of arms. Each pair is identical except for ONE knob
; that the combat model says MUST move one of the three headline numbers, and the
; encounter itself is a shared fragment so there is no second copy to drift:
;
;   hitrate  <- DEX             accuracy is always DEX (docs/combat.md)
;   dealt    <- weapon skill    damage scales with (1 + skill_damage x level)
;   taken    <- monster strength a different COUNTING SITE, not just a different
;                                number: `dealt` is summed at MonsterTarget::Wound
;                                and `taken` at PartyTarget::Wound, so one can go
;                                dead while the other still reports
;
; MEASURED SEPARATIONS, which is what the thresholds are set from rather than
; guessed at: hitrate 0.336 -> 0.668 (2.0x), dealt/swing 11.02 -> 17.84 (1.6x),
; taken 19.2 -> 185.1 (9.6x, and the two arms' ranges do not come close to
; overlapping).
;
; The `taken` figures fell from 234.4 when ClearWipeLatch stopped handing the
; monsters a stored swing on the frame a wipe is healed: these arms wipe the
; party often, so several samples had been collecting one free blow each. The
; thresholds were loose enough to absorb it, which is the argument for setting
; them from a separation rather than from the number itself.
;
; Each arm sweeps its encounter over several seeds, because combat is dice and
; one sample is an anecdote — a 5% fumble decides a single fight.
;
; THE ASSERTIONS LIVE IN Eval.ps1, with the thresholds and the reasoning for
; each. They are deliberately loose: this is a check that the instrument is
; ALIVE, not a balance test. A number that has stopped responding fails it; a
; number that responds differently after a balance change does not.

logecho on

; ---- pair 1: hitrate must respond to DEX ---------------------------------
; Measured 0.307 at dex 5 against 0.630 at dex 25 - a 2x separation, which is
; what makes a 1.3x threshold safe.

echo ARM dex-low
reset
timescale 0
lockstep on
autoattack on
setstat 0 dex 5
setstat 1 dex 5
setstat 2 dex 5
setstat 3 dex 5
sweep 5 rungs/respond-offence.eval

echo ARM dex-high
reset
timescale 0
lockstep on
autoattack on
setstat 0 dex 25
setstat 1 dex 25
setstat 2 dex 25
setstat 3 dex 25
sweep 5 rungs/respond-offence.eval

; ---- pair 2: dealt-per-swing must respond to WEAPON SKILL ----------------
; Measured 10.58/swing at blade 0 against 19.34 at blade 10. PER SWING, not
; total: a knob that changed the swing RATE would otherwise look like one that
; changed damage.
;
; Both arms carry the same blade, so the weapon is not the variable. (It is worth
; knowing that at skill 0 that blade is WORSE per swing than bare hands - a real
; content finding from the audit, and the reason the low arm is skill 0 with a
; weapon rather than no weapon at all.)

echo ARM skill-low
reset
timescale 0
lockstep on
autoattack on
equip serrated_blade 0 0
equip serrated_blade 1 0
equip serrated_blade 2 0
equip serrated_blade 3 0
sweep 5 rungs/respond-offence.eval

echo ARM skill-high
reset
timescale 0
lockstep on
autoattack on
equip serrated_blade 0 0
equip serrated_blade 1 0
equip serrated_blade 2 0
equip serrated_blade 3 0
setskill 0 blade 10
setskill 1 blade 10
setskill 2 blade 10
setskill 3 blade 10
sweep 5 rungs/respond-offence.eval

; ---- pair 3: taken must respond to how dangerous the monsters are --------
; `taken` is counted at a DIFFERENT SITE from `dealt` (PartyTarget::Wound rather
; than MonsterTarget::Wound), so it needs its own arm — one can go dead while the
; other reports perfectly.
;
; This is the one pair whose knob is on the MONSTER, so it is the one pair that
; needs two fragment files; both say so at the top. The first version of this
; used armour, which shares a fragment and reads better, and it was dropped
; because it moved `taken` by 17% against noise of the same size.

echo ARM threat-low
reset
timescale 0
lockstep on
autoattack on
sweep 5 rungs/respond-defence-weak.eval

echo ARM threat-high
reset
timescale 0
lockstep on
autoattack on
sweep 5 rungs/respond-defence-strong.eval

; The defence arms leave the party wiped as often as not, and a script that ends
; out of play is a failure now (docs/eval-audit.md F19). This is the one place
; that rule needs an explicit answer rather than a fix.
heal
state
